wireguard-mc
============

This package provides a customized wireguard-go that speaks WireGuard over
TCP with optional Minecraft protocol camouflage. Both tunnel ends must use
this binary (not kernel WireGuard / stock UDP wireguard-go).

Key generation (same as wg)
---------------------------

  umask 077
  wireguard-go genkey | tee private.key | wireguard-go pubkey > public.key
  wireguard-go genpsk > psk.key

Quick start — Master (control plane)
------------------------------------

Master owns the mesh table and pushes desired config to agents. A host
installed as master cannot also run an agent tunnel.

1. sudo wireguard-go install master
2. Edit /etc/wireguard/wireguard-go-master.json (adminPassword, listen, dataDir)
3. systemctl restart wireguard-go-master
4. Open http://HOST:8443/ — login, create nodes/links/forwards

Agent nodes only need:

  /etc/wireguard/wireguard-go-agent.json
  {
    "masterUrl": "http://MASTER:8443",
    "key": "<enrollToken or node API key>"
  }

Agent local file only stores masterUrl + key. All other settings (interface,
poll interval, transport/MC camouflage, peers, forwards) are pushed by Master
via /api/agent/config and stored in Master's SQLite database (mesh.db).
After first enroll, key is replaced with the node token automatically.

Then: sudo wireguard-go install

Transport (MC camouflage) remains local:
  /etc/wireguard/wireguard-go-transport.json

Legacy wg0.conf is disabled by default. Set WG_LEGACY_CONF=1 to load it.

Quick start — legacy wg0.conf (optional)
----------------------------------------

1. Edit /etc/wireguard/wireguard-go-transport.json
   (loginPluginSecret must match on both ends).
2. Copy and edit the interface config:
     cp /etc/wireguard/wg0.conf.example /etc/wireguard/wg0.conf
     chmod 600 /etc/wireguard/wg0.conf
3. Enable and start with WG_LEGACY_CONF=1, or keep using conf only when
   wireguard-go-agent.json is absent and env is set on the unit.
4. Status / logs:
     systemctl status wireguard-go@wg0
     journalctl -u wireguard-go@wg0 -f

After apt upgrade, all enabled/active wireguard-go@ instances are restarted
automatically so the new binary is picked up.

Stop and remove the service unit instance:
     sudo wireguard-go uninstall
     sudo wireguard-go uninstall --purge   # also delete configs
     sudo wireguard-go uninstall master [--purge]

install / uninstall auto-elevate when not root: try `sudo -n` (silent if
credentials are cached), then `pkexec` (desktop prompt), then interactive
`sudo`. On Windows, `install`/`uninstall` trigger a UAC elevation (runas)
and register a LocalSystem service named wireguard-go-<iface>.

Foreground (debug):
     sudo LOG_LEVEL=verbose /usr/bin/wireguard-go -f wg0
     sudo /usr/bin/wireguard-go master

Binary path: /usr/bin/wireguard-go
Config dir:  /etc/wireguard/
Unit files:  /lib/systemd/system/wireguard-go@.service
             /lib/systemd/system/wireguard-go-master.service

Docker
------

Release workflow can build/push a multi-arch image (amd64+arm64) to GHCR when
workflow_dispatch input "build_docker" is checked, or when repository Variable
ENABLE_DOCKER_RELEASE=true. Optional Variable DOCKER_IMAGE overrides the image
name (default ghcr.io/<owner>/<repo>).

Builds publish binaries/.deb directly to the GitHub Release (and Docker tags to
GHCR). Actions Artifacts are not used, so artifact storage quota does not apply.

Local build:

  bash scripts/package-docker.sh
  # IMAGE=ghcr.io/OWNER/REPO bash scripts/package-docker.sh --push

Run (host network + TUN):

  mkdir -p config
  cp /path/to/wg0.conf config/wg0.conf
  cp /path/to/wireguard-go-transport.json config/
  docker compose up -d

Or:

  docker run --rm --network host --cap-add=NET_ADMIN --cap-add=NET_RAW \
    --device=/dev/net/tun \
    -v "$PWD/config:/etc/wireguard" \
    ghcr.io/OWNER/REPO:TAG

Dual-server / TO NAT
--------------------

Three-node overlay on one VPN subnet (example 10.0.0.0/24):

  C (internal-only) --ToNAT--> B (can egress) --Endpoint--> A (public)

  A = 10.0.0.1   B = 10.0.0.2   C = 10.0.0.3

Example configs (installed under /etc/wireguard/):

  wg-nat-a.conf.example   public node
  wg-nat-b.conf.example   NAT gateway / dual-server
  wg-nat-c.conf.example   TO NAT client

Key options:

  [Interface] ToNAT = true
      C-side mode flag. [Peer] is a normal WireGuard peer to B
      (PublicKey, Endpoint, AllowedIPs, …). Announces ToNAT on the wire
      so B auto-marks the peer as NatClient.

  B-side: no NatClient= / NatUpstream= needed.
      Upstream is the peer that has Endpoint. When a ToNAT client connects,
      B auto-registers it (SNAT + nested-block). Add one [Peer] per client
      (PublicKey + AllowedIPs /32) for multi-client.

  Optional overrides: NatUpstream=, NatClient=true|false

AllowedIPs rules of thumb:

  - On A: Peer B AllowedIPs must include B and C (e.g. 10.0.0.2/32, 10.0.0.3/32).
  - On B: Peer A AllowedIPs = only A's VPN IP (e.g. 10.0.0.1/32).
    Do not put 0.0.0.0/0 on A, or C's default route would enter A
    instead of SNAT egress on B.
  - On C: Peer B AllowedIPs = 0.0.0.0/0 (and optionally ::/0).

C must not configure Endpoint to A's public address; B rejects nested
reconnects to that public ListenPort.

Windows (Windows 10+ amd64)
---------------------------

Release binaries are named wireguard-mc-windows10-amd64-<version>.exe.

The exe embeds the official signed wintun.dll and releases it beside itself
on first tunnel start (no manual copy needed). The process needs write
access to the directory containing the .exe.

On first start it also releases wireguard-go-transport.json into the config
directory when missing (same example as the Debian package). Edit
loginPluginSecret so both peers match.

Config directory (override with WG_CONF_DIR):

  %ProgramData%\wireguard\<iface>.conf
  %ProgramData%\wireguard\wireguard-go-transport.json

When NatClient / NatUpstream is enabled on Windows, the NAT gateway enables
IP forwarding (Linux ip_forward equivalent) via:

  1. HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\Tcpip\Parameters\IPEnableRouter=1
  2. Set-NetIPInterface -Forwarding Enabled on interfaces

Run elevated (Administrator). Nested reconnects are still dropped in userspace;
WinNAT / firewall rules are best-effort extras.
